<%# ── MODAL 4: $260B PEAK YEAR 2008 ── %>
<dialog id="peak-year-modal" class="hm-dialog" aria-labelledby="hm-peak-title">
  <div class="hm-header">
    <button class="hm-close" onclick="this.closest('dialog').close()" aria-label="Close">✕</button>
    <div class="hm-eyebrow">◆ Islamic Jihad Cost Database · Peak spending</div>
    <div class="hm-headline" id="hm-peak-title">$260B</div>
    <p class="hm-subtitle">The year the war machine ran at full throttle — 2008</p>
  </div>
  <div class="hm-body">
    <div class="hm-stats">
      <div class="hm-stat"><div class="hm-stat-val">$16B</div><div class="hm-stat-lbl">Pre-9/11 annual CT baseline (2001)</div></div>
      <div class="hm-stat"><div class="hm-stat-val">16×</div><div class="hm-stat-lbl">Multiple vs. 2001 baseline</div></div>
      <div class="hm-stat"><div class="hm-stat-val">$714M</div><div class="hm-stat-lbl">Spent per day in 2008</div></div>
      <div class="hm-stat"><div class="hm-stat-val">$17.3B</div><div class="hm-stat-lbl">NASA's entire 2008 budget</div></div>
    </div>
    <ul class="hm-facts">
      <li>The 2008 peak captured the Iraq Surge (2007–09) and the Afghanistan ramp-up simultaneously — both wars at full intensity at the same time.</li>
      <li>NASA's entire 2008 budget was $17.3B. At 2008 CT spending rates, that was consumed in 24 days.</li>
      <li>The Iraq Surge worked: violence fell ~90% from its 2007 peak by late 2008. But the model that achieved it cost over $2 billion per week at its height.</li>
      <li>The U.S. has never returned to pre-9/11 CT spending levels. Even after the Afghanistan withdrawal, DHS alone requested $97B in FY2024 — six times the pre-9/11 total CT budget.</li>
      <li>The 16× multiple understates the true expansion: the pre-9/11 number excluded intelligence spending. Including CIA/NSA, the real multiple is closer to 10× — but on a much larger base.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="hm-srcs">
      <div class="hm-srcs-lbl">Sources</div>
      <p class="hm-srcs-list">
        <a href="https://www.stimson.org/2018/counterterrorism-spending-protecting-america-while-promoting-efficiencies-and-accountability/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Stimson Center, Counterterrorism Spending (2018)</a>
        &middot;
        <a href="https://watson.brown.edu/costsofwar/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Brown University, Costs of War Project (2021)</a>
        &middot;
        <a href="https://www.cbo.gov/topics/defense-and-national-security" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Congressional Budget Office baseline data</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="hm-share-bar">
